    <h3>Primary Infrastructure Supported by UTC</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Internet Routing and Protocols (NTP)</strong>: Network Time Protocol (NTP) synchronizes computer clocks over network connections. Local servers query NTP Stratum servers to maintain clock accuracy within milliseconds. NTP uses UTC as its baseline.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Distributed Databases</strong>: Modern relational and document databases (e.g., PostgreSQL, Google Cloud Spanner, CockroachDB) store transaction commits in UTC. If servers used local EST/EDT, the annual transition in November—where the 1:00 AM hour repeats—would result in duplicate timestamps, database key collisions, and out-of-order logs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Satellite Navigation and GPS</strong>: GPS satellites contain atomic clocks. Because GPS tracking depends on comparing nanosecond signals between satellites and receivers, time must be synchronized globally. GPS Time operates on an atomic standard referenced to UTC.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>International Aviation</strong>: To prevent scheduling errors, pilots, flight dispatchers, and air traffic controllers coordinate operations using UTC (Zulu time). If flights crossing continents used local time zones, calculating flight progress and slot coordination would be highly error-prone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Weather Forecasting</strong>: The World Meteorological Organization synchronizes weather observations (such as satellite scans and weather balloon releases) to exact UTC hours, ensuring global weather models are generated from concurrent data.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

