  <section id="common-mistakes" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Mistakes">
    <h2>10. Common Mistakes &amp; Scheduling Pitfalls</h2>
    <p>
      Timezone abbreviations are frequently misused, leading to scheduling conflicts and system bugs:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Confusing UTC with GMT</strong>: While GMT and UTC share the same clock hours, GMT is an astronomical timezone observed by countries, whereas UTC is a scientific standard.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Using EST in Summer</strong>: Scheduling a meeting in July and writing "EST" as the timezone indicates a UTC-5 offset, while Eastern clocks are actually observing EDT (UTC-4).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ignoring Seasonal Shifts</strong>: Hardcoding UTC-5 in database systems for Eastern locations will cause a permanent 1-hour time shift during daylight saving months.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

